var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Reference","page":"Reference","text":"This page documents all symbols provided by LatticeProteins.jl. None of them are exported, so they must be accessed with the LatticeProteins. prefix or imported explicitly (e.g. using LatticeProteins: pnat).","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Structures-and-contact-maps","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Structures and contact maps"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Energy-and-folding-probability","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Energy and folding probability"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Sequence-encoding","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Sequence encoding"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Sampling","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Sampling"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Miyazawa-Jernigan-matrix","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Miyazawa-Jernigan matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Reference-data","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Reference data"},{"category":"module","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.LatticeProteins","page":"Reference","text":"LatticeProteins\n\nSimulate the lattice proteins model on a 3×3×3 cubic lattice in Julia.\n\nA lattice protein is a simplified model where a protein sequence of 27 amino acids folds on a 3×3×3 cubic lattice. The energy of a sequence in a given structure is determined by pairwise contacts using the Miyazawa-Jernigan interaction matrix.\n\nReferences\n\nJacquin, Hugo, et al. \"Benchmarking inverse statistical approaches for protein structure and design with exactly solvable models.\" PLoS computational biology 12.5 (2016): e1004889.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.LatticeProteins"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.CONTACT_MAPS","page":"Reference","text":"CONTACT_MAPS\n\nPre-loaded contact maps for all 10,000 compact structures. An Array{Int, 3} of size (28, 10000, 2). Use CONTACT_MAPS[:, s, :] to get the contact pairs for structure s. See load_contact_maps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.CONTACT_MAPS"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.N_STRUCTURES","page":"Reference","text":"N_STRUCTURES\n\nTotal number of pre-computed compact structures (10,000).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.N_STRUCTURES"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.N_CONTACTS","page":"Reference","text":"N_CONTACTS\n\nNumber of pairwise contacts in the 3×3×3 cubic lattice (28).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.N_CONTACTS"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.L","page":"Reference","text":"L\n\nSequence length for lattice proteins on the 3×3×3 cubic lattice (27).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.L"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.CONTACT_MAP_A","page":"Reference","text":"CONTACT_MAP_A\n\nIndex of reference structure A (625) from Jacquin et al. (2016).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.CONTACT_MAP_A"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.CONTACT_MAP_B","page":"Reference","text":"CONTACT_MAP_B\n\nIndex of reference structure B (848) from Jacquin et al. (2016).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.CONTACT_MAP_B"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.CONTACT_MAP_C","page":"Reference","text":"CONTACT_MAP_C\n\nIndex of reference structure C (6683) from Jacquin et al. (2016).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.CONTACT_MAP_C"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.CONTACT_MAP_D","page":"Reference","text":"CONTACT_MAP_D\n\nIndex of reference structure D (6685) from Jacquin et al. (2016).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.CONTACT_MAP_D"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.load_contact_maps","page":"Reference","text":"load_contact_maps()\n\nLoad the pre-computed contact maps for 10,000 compact structures on the 3×3×3 cubic lattice. Returns an Array{Int, 3} of size (28, 10000, 2), where CONTACT_MAPS[:, s, :] gives the 28 pairs of contacting residue indices for structure s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.load_contact_maps"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.energy","page":"Reference","text":"energy(cm::Int, seq::AbstractVector{Int})\n\nCompute the contact energy of amino acid sequence seq in structure cm.\n\nThe energy is the sum of Miyazawa-Jernigan interaction energies over all contacting residue pairs in the given structure. seq must be a vector of length 27 with integer amino acid indices in 1:20. cm is the structure index in 1:10000 (see N_STRUCTURES).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.energy"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.pnat","page":"Reference","text":"pnat(cm::Int, seq::AbstractVector{Int})\n\nProbability that sequence seq folds into structure cm. Equivalent to exp(log_pnat(cm, seq)).\n\nSee also log_pnat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.pnat"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.log_pnat","page":"Reference","text":"log_pnat(cm::Int, seq::AbstractVector{Int})\n\nCompute the log-probability that sequence seq folds into structure cm (the \"native\" structure), as opposed to any of the other pre-computed structures.\n\nThe probability is computed using a Boltzmann distribution at temperature 1 over the 10,000 pre-computed compact structures:\n\nlog P_textnat(textcm mid textseq) = -log sum_s expbigl(E(textcm textseq) - E(s textseq)bigr)\n\nSee also pnat, energy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.log_pnat"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.AMINO_ACIDS","page":"Reference","text":"AMINO_ACIDS\n\nAmino acid alphabet string used for encoding: \"CMFILVWYAGTSNQDEHRKP-\" (20 amino acids plus a gap character). This ordering follows the convention used in the lattice proteins literature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.AMINO_ACIDS"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.onehot","page":"Reference","text":"onehot(seq::AbstractString)\n\nOne-hot encode an amino acid sequence string. Returns a BitMatrix of size (21, L), where L is the length of seq. Each column is a one-hot vector over the AMINO_ACIDS alphabet.\n\nonehot(seqs::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString})\n\nOne-hot encode a vector of amino acid sequence strings. All sequences must have the same length L. Returns a BitArray{3} of size (21, L, N), where N = length(seqs).\n\nonehot(A::Union{AbstractVector{Int}, AbstractMatrix{Int}})\n\nOne-hot encode integer-coded amino acid sequences (values in 1:21).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.onehot"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.potts","page":"Reference","text":"potts(X::BitMatrix)\npotts(X::BitArray{3})\n\nConvert a one-hot encoded representation to integer-coded (Potts) representation by taking the argmax along the first dimension.\n\npotts(s::Union{AbstractString, AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}})\n\nConvert amino acid string(s) to integer-coded (Potts) representation, using the AMINO_ACIDS alphabet ordering.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.potts"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.aaseq","page":"Reference","text":"aaseq(X::Union{BitMatrix, BitArray{3}})\n\nConvert a one-hot encoded representation back to amino acid sequence string(s). X must have 21 rows (one per amino acid in AMINO_ACIDS).\n\naaseq(P::AbstractVector{Int})\n\nConvert an integer-coded sequence to an amino acid string.\n\naaseq(P::AbstractMatrix{Int})\n\nConvert a matrix of integer-coded sequences (each column is one sequence) to a vector of amino acid strings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.aaseq"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.metropolis!","page":"Reference","text":"metropolis!(seq::AbstractVector{Int}, cm::Int; β=1, nsteps=1)\n\nPerform Metropolis Monte Carlo sampling on the amino acid sequence seq, targeting structure cm. The sequence is mutated in place.\n\nAt each step, a random position is selected and mutated to a random amino acid (1–20). The move is accepted or rejected using the Metropolis criterion with inverse temperature β, where the energy is -log_pnat(cm, seq).\n\nArguments\n\nseq: integer-coded amino acid sequence of length 27 (values in 1:20).\ncm: target structure index.\nβ: inverse temperature (higher values favor sequences that fold into cm).\nnsteps: number of Monte Carlo steps to perform.\n\nReturns the mutated seq.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.metropolis!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.random_msa","page":"Reference","text":"random_msa(cm::Int; nseqs=1, β=1, nsteps=1000)\n\nGenerate a random multiple sequence alignment (MSA) of nseqs sequences that fold into structure cm, using Metropolis Monte Carlo sampling.\n\nEach sequence is initialized randomly and then evolved for nsteps Metropolis steps at inverse temperature β. Returns a Matrix{Int} of size (27, nseqs), where each column is an integer-coded amino acid sequence.\n\nSee also metropolis!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.random_msa"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.MJ","page":"Reference","text":"MJ\n\nThe 20×20 Miyazawa-Jernigan contact energy matrix (preloaded as Matrix{Float64}). See load_miyazawa_jernigan_matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.MJ"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.load_miyazawa_jernigan_matrix","page":"Reference","text":"load_miyazawa_jernigan_matrix([T=Float64])\n\nLoad the 20×20 Miyazawa-Jernigan contact energy matrix from the bundled data file. Element (i, j) gives the interaction energy between amino acid types i and j, where the amino acid indexing follows the order in AMINO_ACIDS (first 20 characters, excluding the gap character).\n\nReturns a Matrix{T}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.load_miyazawa_jernigan_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.load_miyazawa_jernigan_aminoacids","page":"Reference","text":"load_miyazawa_jernigan_aminoacids()\n\nLoad the amino acid ordering used in the Miyazawa-Jernigan matrix file. Returns a String of single-letter amino acid codes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.load_miyazawa_jernigan_aminoacids"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.Hugo_MSA","page":"Reference","text":"Hugo_MSA(which::Symbol)\n\nLoad a reference MSA dataset from Jacquin et al. (2016) as a Vector{String} of amino acid sequences. which must be one of :A, :B, :C, or :D.\n\nSee also Hugo_MSA_pnat, Hugo_MSA_path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.Hugo_MSA"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.Hugo_MSA_pnat","page":"Reference","text":"Hugo_MSA_pnat(which::Symbol)\n\nLoad the native fold probabilities (pnat values) for the reference MSA dataset from Jacquin et al. (2016). which must be one of :A, :B, :C, or :D. Returns a Vector{Float64}.\n\nSee also Hugo_MSA.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.Hugo_MSA_pnat"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#LatticeProteins.Hugo_MSA_path","page":"Reference","text":"Hugo_MSA_path(which::Symbol)\n\nReturn the file path to one of the reference MSA datasets from Jacquin et al. (2016). which must be one of :A, :B, :C, or :D, corresponding to the four reference structures (CONTACT_MAP_A, etc.).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LatticeProteins.Hugo_MSA_path"},{"category":"section","location":"#LatticeProteins.jl","page":"Home","text":"Simulate the lattice proteins model on a 3×3×3 cubic lattice in Julia.\n\nIn this model, a protein is represented as a chain of 27 amino acids that folds on a 3×3×3 cubic lattice. The energy of a sequence in a given structure is determined by pairwise contacts between amino acids using the Miyazawa-Jernigan interaction matrix. The probability that a sequence folds into a given \"native\" structure is computed via a Boltzmann distribution over 10,000 pre-computed compact structures.\n\nThis package does not export any symbols; everything is accessed either with the LatticeProteins. prefix or by importing the names explicitly, e.g. using LatticeProteins: pnat, energy.","title":"LatticeProteins.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"This package is registered. Install it with:\n\nimport Pkg\nPkg.add(\"LatticeProteins\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using LatticeProteins: pnat, energy, onehot, aaseq, potts, random_msa,\n    CONTACT_MAP_A, Hugo_MSA, Hugo_MSA_pnat\n\n# Compute the probability that a sequence folds into structure A\nseq = potts(\"EKAMPAMDPDMAHEHKKKIRAWMFEGE\")  # convert to integer-coded sequence\np = pnat(CONTACT_MAP_A, seq)\n\n# Compute the contact energy\nE = energy(CONTACT_MAP_A, seq)\n\n# Generate sequences that fold into structure A via Metropolis sampling\nmsa = random_msa(CONTACT_MAP_A; nseqs=100, β=100, nsteps=1000)\n\nSee the Tutorial for a worked walkthrough and the Reference for the full list of functions.","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contents","page":"Home","text":"Pages = [\"literate/tutorial.md\", \"reference.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citation","page":"Home","text":"If you use this package, please cite:\n\nFernandez-de-Cossio-Diaz, Jorge, Clément Roussel, Simona Cocco, and Remi Monasson. \"Accelerated Sampling with Stacked Restricted Boltzmann Machines.\" The Twelfth International Conference on Learning Representations (2024).","title":"Citation"},{"category":"section","location":"#References","page":"Home","text":"Jacquin, Hugo, et al. \"Benchmarking inverse statistical approaches for protein structure and design with exactly solvable models.\" PLoS computational biology 12.5 (2016): e1004889.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"literate/tutorial/#Tutorial","page":"Tutorial","text":"This tutorial walks through the main features of LatticeProteins.jl: encoding amino acid sequences, computing contact energies and folding probabilities, and sampling sequences that fold into a target structure.","title":"Tutorial"},{"category":"section","location":"literate/tutorial/#Setup","page":"Tutorial","text":"Since the package does not export any symbols, we import the names we need explicitly. (Alternatively, everything is reachable through the LatticeProteins. prefix.)\n\nusing LatticeProteins: pnat, log_pnat, energy, onehot, aaseq, potts, AMINO_ACIDS,\n    metropolis!, random_msa, CONTACT_MAP_A, CONTACT_MAP_B,\n    CONTACT_MAPS, N_STRUCTURES, MJ, Hugo_MSA, Hugo_MSA_pnat\n\nA lattice protein is a chain of 27 amino acids folded onto a 3×3×3 cubic lattice. The package ships with 10,000 pre-computed compact structures (contact maps):\n\nN_STRUCTURES\n\nFour of these structures are highlighted in Jacquin et al. (2016) and exposed as constants CONTACT_MAP_A, CONTACT_MAP_B, CONTACT_MAP_C and CONTACT_MAP_D. Each is simply an index into the structure database:\n\nCONTACT_MAP_A","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"literate/tutorial/#Encoding-sequences","page":"Tutorial","text":"Sequences are written as strings over the AMINO_ACIDS alphabet (20 amino acids plus a gap character):\n\nAMINO_ACIDS\n\nMost numerical routines work with integer-coded (Potts) sequences, where each amino acid is replaced by its index in the alphabet. Use potts to convert:\n\nseq = potts(\"EKAMPAMDPDMAHEHKKKIRAWMFEGE\")\n\nWe can convert back to a string with aaseq:\n\naaseq(seq)\n\nA sequence can also be one-hot encoded into a 21 × 27 BitMatrix with onehot, which is convenient for machine-learning workflows:\n\nX = onehot(\"EKAMPAMDPDMAHEHKKKIRAWMFEGE\")\nsize(X)","title":"Encoding sequences"},{"category":"section","location":"literate/tutorial/#Energy-and-folding-probability","page":"Tutorial","text":"The contact energy of a sequence in a given structure is the sum of Miyazawa-Jernigan interaction energies over all contacting residue pairs:\n\nenergy(CONTACT_MAP_A, seq)\n\nThe same sequence has a different energy in a different structure:\n\nenergy(CONTACT_MAP_B, seq)\n\nThe probability that a sequence folds into a particular \"native\" structure is given by a Boltzmann distribution over all 10,000 competing structures. This is pnat (and its logarithm log_pnat):\n\npnat(CONTACT_MAP_A, seq)\n\nlog_pnat(CONTACT_MAP_A, seq)","title":"Energy and folding probability"},{"category":"section","location":"literate/tutorial/#Sampling-sequences-that-fold","page":"Tutorial","text":"We can search for sequences that fold into a target structure using Metropolis Monte Carlo. random_msa starts from random sequences and evolves each one at inverse temperature β; a large β strongly favours sequences with high folding probability for the target structure.\n\nmsa = random_msa(CONTACT_MAP_A; nseqs = 20, β = 100, nsteps = 200)\nsize(msa)\n\nEach column of the returned matrix is an integer-coded sequence. The sampled sequences fold into structure A with high probability:\n\nusing Statistics: mean\nmean(pnat(CONTACT_MAP_A, col) for col in eachcol(msa))\n\nmetropolis! exposes the same sampler for a single sequence, mutating it in place:\n\ns = potts(\"EKAMPAMDPDMAHEHKKKIRAWMFEGE\")\nmetropolis!(s, CONTACT_MAP_A; β = 100, nsteps = 200)\npnat(CONTACT_MAP_A, s)","title":"Sampling sequences that fold"},{"category":"section","location":"literate/tutorial/#Reference-datasets","page":"Tutorial","text":"The package also bundles the reference multiple sequence alignments (MSAs) and their folding probabilities from Jacquin et al. (2016), accessible with Hugo_MSA and Hugo_MSA_pnat:\n\nsequences = Hugo_MSA(:A)\nlength(sequences)\n\npnat_values = Hugo_MSA_pnat(:A)\nfirst(pnat_values, 5)\n\nSee the Reference for the complete list of available functions.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Reference datasets"}]
}
